{"command": "cat >> /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md <<'EOF'\n\n## Requirements *(mandatory)*\n\n### Functional Requirements\n\n- **FR-001**: The system MUST provide a batch writer that wraps a supplied TinyDB instance additively, without changing the observable behavior of that instance's ordinary APIs.\n- **FR-002**: The batch writer MUST operate on the default table only.\n- **FR-003**: The batch writer MUST expose an apply operation that accepts a list of operation dictionaries, where each operation is either an insert (carrying a 'document' that is a dict, possibly empty) or a remove (carrying a 'doc_id' that is a positive integer greater than 0 and not a boolean).\n- **FR-004**: The apply operation MUST return the list of inserted document IDs in insertion order; remove operations MUST contribute no result; an empty input list MUST return [].\n- **FR-005**: The apply operation MUST raise ValueError for unknown or malformed operations (unknown or missing op, missing or non-dict 'document', missing or non-positive or boolean 'doc_id', non-dict operation entry) and MUST raise ValueError for a non-list operations argument.\n- **FR-006**: The batch writer MUST validate every operation structurally before committing any change to the default table; no partial commit may be observable from any failure.\n- **FR-007**: Batch application MUST be atomic: operations are applied sequentially, and on any error (including a remove targeting a missing ID) the complete pre-batch default-table data MUST be restored.\n- **FR-008**: A remove targeting a missing ID MUST be treated as an error (and therefore trigger rollback).\n- **FR-009**: Previously acquired handles to the default table MUST reflect both successful application and rollback without re-acquisition.\n- **FR-010**: Ordinary TinyDB reads and writes, including preexisting documents, MUST continue to work alongside the writer, and direct inserts performed between batch calls MUST be visible to the writer (no stale initial snapshot).\n- **FR-011**: Inserted data and returned data MUST NOT alias the caller's input: nested structures are stored independently, and mutating a caller's document or operation after apply MUST NOT mutate stored data.\n- **FR-012**: The batch writer MUST expose a preview operation that accepts the same operation-list input as apply and returns exactly the inserted IDs a successful apply of that batch would produce.\n- **FR-013**: Preview MUST leave the default-table data, the next insertion ID, and all existing table handles and query caches unchanged, including when the previewed batch would fail (in which case preview raises ValueError).\n- **FR-014**: Preview MUST return a new, detached list on every call; mutating a previously returned preview list MUST NOT affect subsequent preview results or the writer's internal state.\n- **FR-015**: Neither preview nor a failed apply MUST consume or advance the next insertion ID; IDs that a failed batch would have used MUST never be reissued to different documents.\n- **FR-016**: Every successful apply MUST advance the next-insertion-ID state and persist that state to the underlying table so that subsequent inserts, whether via the writer or direct database writes, continue the ID sequence without reuse or rewind, even after remove operations.\n- **FR-017**: The apply operation MUST accept an optional idempotency token that is either absent/None (normal operation) or a nonempty string; any other token value MUST raise ValueError without changing data.\n- **FR-018**: Reapplying an identical batch with a token that already recorded a successful apply MUST return a detached copy of the original inserted IDs without re-running the mutations, even if other direct database writes occurred in the meantime.\n- **FR-019**: Applying a different batch with a token already bound to a successful batch MUST raise ValueError without changing data.\n- **FR-020**: A failed batch MUST NOT consume or bind its token; the same token may be retried after failure as if unused.\n- **FR-021**: Token bookkeeping MUST be internal to the batch writer instance and MUST never be written into user documents; preview MUST never record or consume a token and MUST keep its original single-argument signature; every returned list MUST be a plain, freely mutable copy that does not expose internal state.\n\n### Key Entities\n\n- **BatchWriter**: The wrapper bound to one TinyDB instance; exposes apply (with optional idempotency token) and preview.\n- **Operation**: A dict describing one change: 'insert' with a 'document' (dict, possibly empty) or 'remove' with a 'doc_id' (positive integer, not boolean); no extra fields allowed.\n- **Document**: A dict stored in the default table, identified by a positive integer ID.\n- **Next insertion ID**: The allocator position for new document IDs; advanced by successful batches and direct writes, never rewound or reused.\n- **Idempotency token**: A nonempty string binding a successful batch to its original result, kept privately by the writer instance.\n\n## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*\n\n### Measurable Outcomes\n\n- **SC-001**: 100% of valid batches (mixed inserts/removes, empty, single-op, empty-document inserts) return exactly the inserted IDs in insertion order and leave the default table in the expected state.\n- **SC-002**: 100% of failing batches (unknown op, malformed fields, boolean/zero/negative doc_id, extra keys, non-list input, missing-ID remove) raise ValueError and leave the default table and the next insertion ID equal to their pre-batch state, as observed through previously acquired table handles.\n- **SC-003**: 100% of post-apply mutations of caller-supplied documents or operations leave stored data unchanged (zero aliased fields observed in tests).\n- **SC-004**: 100% of preview calls return exactly the IDs a matching successful apply would return while leaving data, the next insertion ID, handles, and query caches unchanged; mutating returned preview lists has zero effect on later previews or internal state.\n- **SC-005**: Across any interleaving of successful/failed batches and direct inserts, the ID sequence is strictly continuing with zero reuses and zero rewinds, including after removes.\n- **SC-006**: 100% of repeated identical batch/token applies return the original IDs with zero additional mutations (table size and contents unchanged by the repeat), and 100% of token mismatches raise ValueError with zero data change; failed batches consume zero tokens.\n- **SC-007**: Ordinary TinyDB reads/writes and preexisting documents show zero behavioral regressions in the project's existing and public acceptance tests.\n- **SC-008**: The feature is delivered without modifying any upstream test file (zero upstream test files changed).\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- **A1**: The supplied TinyDB instance is the only storage target; the writer does not create, open, or configure storage.\n- **A2**: \"Positive integer\" for 'doc_id' means an integer greater than 0; booleans are not accepted as IDs (boolean check precedes integer check).\n- **A3**: The result of a successful apply or preview is a plain Python list of IDs (not a database query object), freshly allocated on each call.\n- **A4**: The error type for all invalid-operation, invalid-token, missing-ID, and token-mismatch conditions is ValueError, as stated in the requirements.\n- **A5**: Operation dicts are validated strictly: an insert operation carries exactly the keys 'op' and 'document'; a remove operation carries exactly the keys 'op' and 'doc_id'. Any extra/unsupported key is malformed (supersedes the earlier milestone-1 assumption that extra keys were ignored; R04 is the controlling requirement).\n- **A6**: Operation order within a batch is significant (sequential semantics); removing an ID inserted earlier in the same batch is valid.\n- **A7**: \"Restore the complete pre-batch default-table data\" means document contents, IDs, and ordering are identical to the pre-batch state.\n- **A8**: \"Identical batch\" for token comparison means the same operations in the same order with equal operation payloads (insert documents deeply equal; remove IDs equal), independent of the caller's list or dict objects.\n- **A9**: The idempotency result for a repeated token is the original inserted IDs from the first successful apply, not newly computed IDs; the repeat performs no mutations at all.\n- **A10**: Token bookkeeping is per writer instance: two writers wrapping the same database do not share token state (no requirement demands sharing).\n- **A11**: The next insertion ID is a property of the underlying default table state; the writer persists it there so direct database writes and other observers see a consistent allocator position.\n\n## Change History\n\n- **Milestone 1 (R01-R03)**: initial apply, atomicity, and aliasing requirements. All retained.\n- **Milestone 2 (R04-R06, plus R05b and the R05 clarification)**: strict pre-commit validation including boolean rejection; preview with zero side effects and detached results; no stale snapshots; ID-continuity guarantee across mixed usage. All retained.\n- **Milestone 3 (R07-R08)**: optional idempotency tokens on apply with per-instance bookkeeping. Retains R01-R06.\n- **Conflicting evidence resolution**: an older generated note claimed \"preview may consume IDs and bool IDs are accepted.\" That note is unverified and is superseded by R04 (bool doc_id rejected) and R05 (preview must not consume IDs); this spec follows R04/R05. A previously passing happy-path smoke test does not verify these boundaries; the acceptance scenarios above (User Stories 3-5) do.\n- **Superseded assumption**: milestone-1 assumption \"extra keys on an operation dict are ignored\" is superseded by R04's strict structural validation (see A5).\nEOF\necho OK3", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "OK3\n"}